  "summary": "Boxing Day streets flood with crepe-paper warriors, cowbell rhythm and goat-skin drums.",
  "description": "Junkanoo is the Bahamas' national carnival, performed on Bay Street, Nassau at Boxing Day and New Year dawn: troupes (A-Side, B-Side) of hundreds spend all year building colossal costumes from cardboard, wire and layered crepe paper — feathered A-giants, dancing suns, themed floats riding flat-bed trucks — while goat-skin drummers, cowbellers and brass horns drive the rushin' rhythm. Its documented roots lie in 18th-century slave holidays honoring John Canoe, a West African figure; emancipation, lobster-fisherman days and tourism-era competitions shaped it further. The artistry is judged like sport: theme coherence, choreography, music and crepe-fringe engineering are all scored.",
  "culturalContext": "Junkanoo — born of 18th-century slave holidays honoring John Canoe — became the Bahamas' national carnival and identity engine: troupe rivalries (Valley Boys, Saxons) structure Nassau social life, crepe-paper artistry is judged like sport, and the Boxing Day rush is the nation's single most unifying event.",
